{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Let the waters swarm.</b> After He completed the form of the heavens, which were made matter for the things that are in the heavens, and the form of the earth, which is matter for the things that are in the earth, He now decreed upon the waters that they become matter for some possessors of the locomotive soul, and said נפש חיה — a living soul — that is, a soul in which there is vitality: these are the fish of the sea, and also the birds that were created from water and from dust. From water — as it is mentioned here: ״ישרצו המים שרץ נפש חיה ועוף יעופף על הארץ״; from dust — as it is written: ״ויצר ה׳ אלהים מן האדמה כל חית השדה ואת כל עוף השמים״. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: birds were created from רקק — meaning רקק of water and dust. And since there is a soul in which there is no vitality at all, namely the vegetative soul, therefore He mentioned here, regarding the possessors of the locomotive soul, נפש חיה — a soul in which there is vitality, for the vegetative soul has no vitality in it; but it is called נפש because just as the possessors of the locomotive soul do not move except by means of a soul, so too the plant does not grow except by means of a soul. Or one may explain that it is not called נפש at all, but rather is called the growing power; and on this basis you will not find on the third day any mention of נפש at all — neither נפש חיה nor even an unqualified נפש." "<b>And know</b> that the movements of all possessors of the locomotive soul are five movements: ascending movement, intermediate movement, lower movement, breaking movement, and creeping movement. Ascending movement is that of the bird, whose movement is to ascend; intermediate movement is the movement of that which swims between heaven and earth and between water and water; lower movement is that of the crawler; breaking movement is that which walks on two; creeping movement is that which goes on its belly. And because of this, the day on which the living soul emerged to move in its movements is called the fifth day (יום חמישי)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:26:27.552197249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}